}4 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}97 & -72 \\\\ -72 & 97\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=13$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=169,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-169I & =[\\begin{aligned}97 & -72 \\\\ -72 & 97\\end{aligned}]-169[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-72 & -72 \\\\ -72 & -72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-169I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}97 & -72 \\\\ -72 & 97\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}72 & -72 \\\\ -72 & 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418153 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418153 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418153 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418153 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b4092bd-68de-5340-8ca8-436b2e1233eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418153 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{30}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418154 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "345be953-89e4-5540-a9e1-d54a38da924f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418154 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-9I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A.$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418155 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418155 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418155 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Σ$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- $U$ is a diagonal matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418155 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d3ee109-94d4-53ed-8971-ac2ef97dce27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418155 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $3\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix,the matrix $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $U$ must be orthogonal but not necessarily diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70b4f245-e7d8-5908-9e68-f829b0901af8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9020d5f8-c5f3-54dc-9474-2d2b14ec7c2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418158 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418158 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418158 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b33c0902-677a-58a2-8ff6-887b643a37e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418158 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=6.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\fr